\chapter{Introduction}

LineScope is a small program, for macOS and for Windows, for \emph{looking} at what resampling and filtering do to an
image. You open an image, derive copies from it (blurred, sharpened, shrunk with nearest-neighbor,
enlarged with Lanczos, and so on), and draw a line across it. For every copy the program shows three
things side by side:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the image itself, at its true pixel scale, with the line drawn on it;
\item the \emph{profile}: one color channel sampled along the line;
\item the \emph{spectrum}: the Fourier transform of that profile.
\end{enumerate}
Because the line is shared, moving it in one copy moves it in all of them, and the differences
between the methods become visible at once. Aliasing shows as false energy below the Nyquist
frequency, over-smoothing as a spectrum that dies too early, and ringing as ripples beside an edge.

\section{How to read this document}

This is a \emph{literate program} in the sense of Donald Knuth~\cite{knuth84}: a document written
for people, from which the program for the machine is extracted. The code appears in
\emph{chunks}. Each chunk has a name and a number, and is written
\[
  \langle\textit{name}\ \textsf{\small n}\rangle \equiv
\]
where it is defined. A chunk may use other chunks by name; that is how a long function is explained
a piece at a time, in the order that makes the ideas clear rather than the order the compiler
wants. When the same name is defined again, the new text is appended to the old, and the header
reads $+\!\equiv$. Below each chunk, a note tells where it is continued and where it is used.

The chunk index at the end of the book lists every chunk.

Two tools turn this document into something useful:
\begin{description}[style=nextline]
\item[Tangling] follows the references from each \emph{root} chunk and writes the source file the
  chunk is named after. The roots of this web are exactly the source files of the project, so
  tangling rebuilds LineScope from this document.
\item[Weaving] produces the \LaTeX{} you are reading, with the cross-references.
\end{description}
The directory [[LiterateP]] holds the web ([[chapters/*.nw]]), the tool [[lit.py]] that does
both jobs using Norman Ramsey's noweb notation~\cite{ramsey94}, and a [[Makefile]]. Running
[[make check]] tangles the web and compares every file, byte for byte, with the real sources in
the repository. So this document can't drift away from the program without the check noticing.

\section{The shape of the program}

The code is split in two.

The first part is [[LineScopeCore]], a Swift package with no user interface. It holds everything
numeric: the pixel buffer (Chapter~\ref{ch:pixels}), resampling (Chapter~\ref{ch:resampling}),
filters (Chapter~\ref{ch:filters}), line sampling, color models and the spectrum
(Chapter~\ref{ch:analysis}), and generated test patterns (Chapter~\ref{ch:patterns}). It is
tested on its own (Chapter~\ref{ch:tests}).

The second part is the app, [[LineScope]]. It holds the document and the per-window session
model (Chapter~\ref{ch:model}), the SwiftUI views (Chapter~\ref{ch:views}) and the menus
(Chapter~\ref{ch:commands}).

This split keeps the mathematics testable without a window, and keeps the views thin: a view
asks the session for a profile or a spectrum and draws it.

These two parts form Part~I of the book, \emph{LineScope for macOS}. Part~II, \emph{LineScope for
Windows}, presents a native port written in C\# with .NET~9 and WPF (Chapter~ef{ch:win-overview}).
The port has the same split, file for file: a UI-free library [[LineScope.Core]]
(Chapter~ef{ch:win-core}) with its tests (Chapter~ef{ch:win-tests}), and a WPF application
with its session model (Chapter~ef{ch:win-model}) and views (Chapter~ef{ch:win-views}). The
numeric algorithms are kept identical, so Part~II doesn't repeat the theory. It concentrates on
what had to change: an FFT of its own in place of Apple's vDSP, CPU versions of the Core Image
filters, and the WPF plumbing for windows, menus and drawing. The root chunks of Part~II are the
files under [[Windows/]], and [[make check]] verifies them in the same way.

\section{Building}

The Xcode project isn't kept in the repository. XcodeGen~\cite{xcodegen} generates it from a
short specification. The app target depends on the local package, is a document \emph{viewer}
for any image type, and is signed ad hoc so it runs without a developer account.

<<project.yml>>=
name: LineScope
options:
  bundleIdPrefix: co.edu.uniandes
  deploymentTarget:
    macOS: "15.0"
packages:
  LineScopeCore:
    path: Packages/LineScopeCore
targets:
  LineScope:
    type: application
    platform: macOS
    sources: [LineScope]
    dependencies:
      - package: LineScopeCore
    info:
      path: LineScope/Info.plist
      properties:
        CFBundleDisplayName: LineScope
        LSApplicationCategoryType: public.app-category.graphics-design
        NSHumanReadableCopyright: ""
        CFBundleDocumentTypes:
          - CFBundleTypeName: Image
            CFBundleTypeRole: Viewer
            LSHandlerRank: Alternate
            LSItemContentTypes: [public.image]
    settings:
      base:
        PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER: co.edu.uniandes.LineScope
        SWIFT_VERSION: "5.0"
        MARKETING_VERSION: "0.1"
        CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION: "1"
        CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY: "-"
        CODE_SIGN_STYLE: Manual
        ENABLE_HARDENED_RUNTIME: NO
        GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE: NO
@ The rank [[Alternate]] means LineScope offers to open images but never claims to be their default
application.

The package manifest declares one library and its tests. One line deserves comment. Swift in a
Debug build is compiled without optimization, and the inner loops of resampling and filtering then
run tens of times slower, since every array access is bounds-checked and nothing is inlined. The
manifest therefore asks for [[-O]] even in Debug. This uses [[unsafeFlags]], which the Swift
Package Manager allows only for local packages like this one.

<<Packages/LineScopeCore/Package.swift>>=
// swift-tools-version: 5.10
import PackageDescription

let package = Package(
    name: "LineScopeCore",
    platforms: [.macOS(.v14)],
    products: [
        .library(name: "LineScopeCore", targets: ["LineScopeCore"]),
    ],
    targets: [
        .target(
            name: "LineScopeCore",
            // Pixel loops are unusably slow at -Onone; optimize even in Debug.
            swiftSettings: [.unsafeFlags(["-O"], .when(configuration: .debug))]
        ),
        .testTarget(
            name: "LineScopeCoreTests",
            dependencies: ["LineScopeCore"]
        ),
    ]
)
@ With these two files in place, the commands are:
\begin{lstlisting}[language={}]
xcodegen generate
xcodebuild -project LineScope.xcodeproj -scheme LineScope build
cd Packages/LineScopeCore && swift test
\end{lstlisting}
